Things are different now than in the 1980s, and the fact that slashem
or spork hasn't taken off to supersede nethack undermines this kind of
argument.  It's not that they are bad variants undeserving of wider
support, development, and improvement - with those things, they could
have eclipsed the stagnant nethack.  But nethack has a well-known name
and web site, and it has r.g.r.n, and it has a community of devoted
followers, some of whom continue to fiercely defend the status quo,
which includes an unreasonable respect for the dev team and their
secretive non-development development model.

From what I've seen, most variant developers hope at best to get into
the club, not create enduring improvements to replace nethack and get
a viable, active game out there for the community again.  There's
always the deference back to Stephenson's group and its methodology
and status.  The dev team encourages this atmosphere by maintaining
the barest suggestion of involvement, dropping rare hints on r.g.r.n,
or responding privately to particular individuals, but not delivering
on the goods.

In that environment, it's not surprising that even smart,
well-thought-out variants have not replaced a stalled core.  The
mindset in the community, particularly the developer community, needs
to change to challenge the dev team.


